@DSL Implicit;
@Behaviour Fcc316LForestRubinSrix;
@Author fem_inhouse project;
@Date 03 / 08 / 2026;
@Description {
  Forest-Rubin SRIX (also published as RubiX) rate-independent FCC single
  crystal plasticity, small strain.

  Samuel Forest and M. B. Rubin, A rate-independent crystal plasticity model
  with a smooth elastic-plastic transition and no slip indeterminacy,
  European Journal of Mechanics A/Solids, volume 55, pages 278 to 288, 2016.
  The DOI is quoted in docs/explanation/forest_rubin_srix.md; MFront parses
  bare DOI strings as malformed numbers, so it is deliberately absent here.

  Everything except the flow rule is shared with Fcc316LMericCailletaud: the
  same twelve octahedral slip systems, the same interaction matrix, the same
  saturating isotropic hardening (tau0, Q, b), the same Armstrong-Frederick
  kinematic hardening (C, d), the same cubic elasticity and the same implicit
  update Dp_s = |Dg_s|.

  The flow rule, equation (7)

  Meric-Cailletaud, rate dependent:

      dg_s/dt = <f_s / K>^n sign(tau_s - X_s)

  SRIX, rate independent:

      dg_s/dt = eqrate <f_s / R> sign(tau_s - X_s)

  where eqrate is the von Mises equivalent rate of the deviatoric total strain,
  equation (8). Incrementally, over a step in which the strain varies linearly:

      Dg_s = Deq <f_s / R> sign(tau_s - X_s),
      Deq  = sqrt(2/3 dev(deto) : dev(deto)).

  Time enters only through Deq, which is itself a strain measure, so the
  response depends on the strain PATH and not on how fast it is traversed.
  There is no dt anywhere below, and test_srix_is_time_independent asserts it
  stays that way.

  Why the local Jacobian is simpler than the Meric-Cailletaud one

  The unknowns of the implicit system are deel and dg; deto is data. Deq is
  therefore a constant during the local Newton iteration, and the flow function
  is linear in f, so d(flow)/df = Deq / R replaces the Norton n v / f.

  That same fact creates an EXPLICIT dependence of fg on deto which the
  StandardElasticity brick does not know about, so the consistent tangent it
  assembles is missing a rank-one correction. The size of that omission is
  measured against finite differences in
  tests/unit/core/test_forest_rubin_srix.py rather than assumed negligible.

  Parameters

  The registered parameter set is the SRIX preset of
  fem_inhouse.core.single_crystal_presets, derived from Nasri and others, 2018.

  R is NOT an identified 316L parameter: it is the analytical transposition of
  the Meric-Cailletaud pair (K, n) through equation (16) at a chosen reference
  strain rate, computed by srix_reference_stress in that same module. The
  default below corresponds to K = 12 MPa, n = 11 and a reference rate of one
  thousandth per second, and it must be overridden once the real strain rate of
  the DIC experiment is documented.
}

@ModellingHypothesis Tridimensional;
@OrthotropicBehaviour;

@Algorithm NewtonRaphson;
@Epsilon 1.e-14;
@Theta 1;

@Brick StandardElasticity{
  young_modulus1 : 99950.29765841035,
  young_modulus2 : 99950.29765841035,
  young_modulus3 : 99950.29765841035,
  poisson_ratio12 : 0.3881987577639752,
  poisson_ratio23 : 0.3881987577639752,
  poisson_ratio13 : 0.3881987577639752,
  shear_modulus12 : 122000.,
  shear_modulus23 : 122000.,
  shear_modulus13 : 122000.
};

@CrystalStructure FCC;
@SlidingSystem<0, 1, -1>{1, 1, 1};
@InteractionMatrix{1.0, 1.0, 0.6, 1.8, 1.6, 12.3, 1.6};

//! Overstress modulus of the SRIX flow rule, equation (7). Replaces (K, n).
@Parameter stress R = 18.7819100705;
R.setEntryName("SrixOverstressModulus");

@Parameter stress tau0 = 40.;
@Parameter stress Q = 10.;
@Parameter real b = 3.;
@Parameter real d = 1500.;
@Parameter stress C = 40000.;

/*!
 * Equivalent strain increment below which the step is treated as producing no
 * slip at all. Guards the rate-independent flow rule against a vanishing or
 * purely hydrostatic increment; see section 4.3 of the specification.
 */
@Parameter strain deqeps = 1.e-14;
deqeps.setEntryName("MinimumEquivalentStrainIncrement");

@StateVariable strain g[Nss];
g.setEntryName("PlasticSlip");

@AuxiliaryStateVariable strain p[Nss];
p.setEntryName("EquivalentPlasticSlip");

@AuxiliaryStateVariable strain a[Nss];
a.setEntryName("BackStrain");

@Integrator {
  using size_type = unsigned short;
  const auto& ss = Fcc316LForestRubinSrixSlipSystems<real>::getSlipSystems();
  const auto& m = ss.him;

  // Equation (8): equivalent measure of the deviatoric total strain increment.
  //
  // It is built from the UNKNOWNS rather than from deto. The elastic residual
  // of the StandardElasticity brick is feel = deel - deto + sum(dg_s m_s), so
  // at convergence deto = deel + sum(dg_s m_s) exactly, and since the Schmid
  // tensors are deviatoric the two readings of Deq are algebraically the same.
  //
  // Writing it this way is what makes the consistent tangent correct. Built
  // from deto, Deq is a constant of the local system and its dependence on the
  // imposed strain is invisible to the brick, which then returns a tangent
  // missing a rank-one term: measured at 2 to 42 percent in established
  // plasticity, and exact only in the elastic range. Built from deel and dg,
  // the same dependence flows through the implicit Jacobian and the brick
  // assembles the right operator with no hand-written @TangentOperator.
  //
  // It also keeps the plane-stress closure honest: every update of the
  // transverse strain changes deel and therefore Deq, so Deq is never frozen on
  // the in-plane components alone.
  auto de = deviator(deel);
  for (size_type i = 0; i != Nss; ++i) {
    de += dg[i] * ss.mus[i];
  }
  const auto deq = sqrt(2 * (de | de) / 3);

  // Section 4.3. A null or purely hydrostatic increment produces no slip. The
  // default residual fg = dg with an identity Jacobian already expresses
  // Dg = 0, and feel = deel - deto left untouched by the brick is the purely
  // elastic answer, so skipping the loop is exactly the required branch. No
  // division by deq is ever reached.
  //
  // This is a guarded block rather than an early return: the StandardElasticity
  // brick appends its own code after this one, and returning from here would
  // skip it.
  if (deq >= deqeps) {
  const auto flow_slope = deq / R;
  // Gradient of Deq with respect to the deviatoric strain increment:
  // dDeq/dDe = (2 / (3 Deq)) De. Already deviatoric, so contracting it with the
  // deviator projector leaves it unchanged and dDeq/ddeel is simply this.
  const auto ndeq = (2 / (3 * deq)) * de;

  real exp_bp[Nss];
  for (size_type i = 0; i != Nss; ++i) {
    const auto p_ = p[i] + theta * abs(dg[i]);
    exp_bp[i] = exp(-b * p_);
  }
  for (size_type i = 0; i != Nss; ++i) {
    const auto tau = sig | ss.mus[i];
    auto r = tau0;
    for (size_type j = 0; j != Nss; ++j) {
      r += Q * m(i, j) * (1 - exp_bp[j]);
    }
    const auto da =  //
        (dg[i] - d * a[i] * abs(dg[i])) / (1 + theta * d * abs(dg[i]));
    const auto x = C * (a[i] + theta * da);
    // Macaulay bracket of equation (7), kept alongside its unclamped argument.
    const auto overstress = abs(tau - x) - r;
    const auto f = max(overstress, stress(0));
    // No overflow guard here: the flow rule is linear in f, unlike the Norton
    // power of Meric-Cailletaud. Rejecting a large f would discard valid states.
    //
    // The slope of the flow rule with respect to f is Deq / R on an active
    // system and ZERO on an inactive one, where the bracket clamps. Unlike
    // Meric-Cailletaud, where dv = n v / f vanishes on its own as f goes to
    // zero, nothing here vanishes by itself: Deq / R is a constant. Writing the
    // active slope on an inactive system corrupts the Jacobian by exactly the
    // number of inactive systems, which makes the local Newton fail
    // non-monotonically in the step size.
    const auto dflow = (overstress > stress(0)) ? flow_slope : 0 * flow_slope;
    const auto sgn = tau - x > 0 ? 1 : -1;
    // elasticity
    feel += dg[i] * ss.mus[i];
    dfeel_ddg(i) = ss.mus[i];
    // rate-independent plasticity
    fg[i] -= flow_slope * f * sgn;
    // Two distinct routes carry the strain increment into this residual: f
    // through the stress, and Deq through the flow amplitude. Differentiating
    // f alone would reproduce the incomplete tangent this formulation exists to
    // avoid, so the Deq route is carried explicitly below as -(f sgn / R) dDeq.
    const auto damplitude = f * sgn / R;
    dfg_ddeel(i) = -dflow * theta * (ss.mus[i] | D) - damplitude * ndeq;
    const auto sgn_gi = dg(i) > 0 ? 1 : -1;
    const auto dda_ddg =
        (1 - d * a[i] * sgn_gi) / (power<2>(1 + theta * d * abs(dg[i])));
    dfg_ddg(i, i) += dflow * C * theta * dda_ddg;
    for (size_type j = 0; j != Nss; ++j) {
      const auto sgn_gj = dg(j) > 0 ? 1 : -1;
      const auto dr = Q * m(i, j) * theta * b * exp_bp[j] * sgn_gj;
      dfg_ddg(i, j) += dflow * dr * sgn - damplitude * (ndeq | ss.mus[j]);
    }
  }
  }  // end of the Deq >= deqeps branch
}

@UpdateAuxiliaryStateVariables {
  using size_type = unsigned short;
  for (size_type i = 0; i != Nss; ++i) {
    // Same implicit hardening update as Meric-Cailletaud: Dp_s = |Dg_s|.
    p[i] += abs(dg[i]);
    const auto da =  //
        (dg[i] - d * a[i] * abs(dg[i])) / (1 + theta * d * abs(dg[i]));
    a[i] += da;
  }
}
